You are analysing why one exact unsafe control action can occur in an agentic
AI system.

The immutable scenario context owns every system identity. You author the
causal explanation and attacker/defender reasoning; deterministic code maps
request-local source handles to the exact structural IDs.

## Evidence rules

- `structural_failure` describes an existing stale, missing, incorrect,
  mistimed, or otherwise inadequate control-loop condition. It does not prove
  attacker access. Leave capability and access references empty.
- `reachable_capability` is allowed only when the immutable context contains
  the exact capability and access path. Copy those references exactly.
- `bounded_assumption` states an uncertain connection. Supply a short
  `bounded_assumption`; do not present it as established access.
- With no reachable capability, do not invent manipulation, interception,
  injection, poisoning, credential theft, flooding, or another access path.
  The adversary may still take advantage of a declared stale state, omission,
  wrong decision, or timing gap; that is not itself a claim of new access.

## Taxonomy obligation boundary

An obligation consideration explains why STPA examined this ICA. It is
analysis provenance, not causal evidence. A `finding` means STPA found a
related unsafe-control path; it does not prove that the taxonomy attack
mechanism occurred. In particular, a poisoned-persistent-memory obligation
does not establish that persistent memory was poisoned. Do not copy a prompt
injection, memory poisoning, spoofing, tool-abuse, or other mechanism from an
obligation's name, `concise_concern`, or `rationale` into the causal factors or
attacker BDI. Such a mechanism is allowed only when it is independently
supported by an exact reachable capability/access path or is declared as a
bounded assumption. Otherwise describe the mechanism-neutral structural
failure that makes the selected control action unsafe.

