## Typed semantic values

When a semantic value is unknown, preserve its type with this exact closed
placeholder shape (do not invent a threshold or runtime field):

```json
{
  "binding_ref": "SEM-1",
  "value_type": "integer",
  "description": "Maximum supported delay in this deployment.",
  "minimum": 0,
  "maximum": null
}
```

`value_type` is one of `integer`, `number`, `string`, or `boolean`.
`minimum` and `maximum` are non-negative numeric bounds for numeric values and
must be `null` for strings and booleans. A placeholder never changes the
condition type, reference, semantic property, or operator.

Allowed condition variants are fixed by the selected UCA: `NOT_PROVIDED`
uses `action_presence(control_action_id, expected: not_provided)`;
`INCORRECT` uses `action_value(control_action_id, property, operator,
expected)` or `state_value(subject_ref, property, operator, expected)`;
`WRONG_TIMING` uses `ordering(reference_step_id, relation)`,
`delay(reference_ref, delay_ms)`, `window(reference_ref, window_from_ms,
window_to_ms)`, or `absence(reference_ref, until_step_id)`; and
`WRONG_DURATION` uses `duration(reference_ref, duration_ms)`. Structural
references must be supplied context identities. The unsafe condition may
repeat the exact selected action only; causal factor sources remain compiler

Only `delay_ms`, `duration_ms`, `window_from_ms`, and `window_to_ms` may use
numeric placeholders. Ordering and absence conditions use only their exact
step/reference identities.
